1
00:00:00,091 --> 00:00:03,008
(intriguing music)

2
00:00:08,430 --> 00:00:09,263
- [Instructor] Hello.

3
00:00:09,263 --> 00:00:12,930
In this video we will discuss preferences.

4
00:00:12,930 --> 00:00:17,100
Preferences are a term
we use, economists use

5
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:22,020
to denote, in a sense, consumers' brains.

6
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:25,410
So consumers' brains generate, of course,

7
00:00:25,410 --> 00:00:29,373
preferences, desires, intentions,

8
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:32,760
levels of satisfaction,

9
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:36,450
and all that is comprised, in a sense,

10
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:39,933
in this broader term we call preferences.

11
00:00:43,170 --> 00:00:47,310
Economists, we assume for simplicity,

12
00:00:47,310 --> 00:00:51,480
that the consumer knows
how different options,

13
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:56,480
how different decisions
will impact their well-being

14
00:00:56,610 --> 00:01:00,720
and that will be able to
choose the best one among those

15
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:03,333
that are available to him or her.

16
00:01:04,770 --> 00:01:07,980
There are many ways to
represent preferences.

17
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:12,400
Imagine we have three
options, apple, banana, mango

18
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:17,280
and in this case, if there
are only three options,

19
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:21,390
apple, banana and mango, some consumer,

20
00:01:21,390 --> 00:01:26,390
our hypothetical consumer,
prefers apple first,

21
00:01:29,880 --> 00:01:33,843
but if it's between mango and banana,

22
00:01:34,770 --> 00:01:38,220
this consumer prefers mango.

23
00:01:38,220 --> 00:01:42,000
So the arrows have a complete description

24
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,130
of this consumer's preferences.

25
00:01:44,130 --> 00:01:46,710
When it's apple versus banana, it's apple.

26
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:49,050
When it's mango versus apple, it's apple.

27
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:51,870
And when it's mango
versus banana, it's mango.

28
00:01:51,870 --> 00:01:54,720
So again, arrows are description,

29
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:56,670
full description of this person,

30
00:01:56,670 --> 00:01:59,553
of this consumer's preferences.

31
00:02:01,140 --> 00:02:05,070
Different consumers could
have different preferences

32
00:02:05,070 --> 00:02:08,580
and the consumer there at the top left,

33
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:11,640
this consumer prefers banana
over the other fruits.

34
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:16,640
The consumer that is at the
bottom is the previous consumer

35
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:22,440
that prefers apple to
the other two fruits.

36
00:02:22,440 --> 00:02:27,440
And the interesting,
more peculiar consumer,

37
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:31,980
top right, prefers apple
when compared to banana,

38
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:34,290
prefers mango when mango
is compared to banana

39
00:02:34,290 --> 00:02:37,110
and prefers banana when mango

40
00:02:37,110 --> 00:02:41,043
and banana are to be contrasted.

41
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:46,500
This last case is what
we call in economics

42
00:02:46,500 --> 00:02:48,450
an inconsistent consumer,

43
00:02:48,450 --> 00:02:52,530
because it does not have
one single best option

44
00:02:52,530 --> 00:02:56,250
in this simple universe of three fruits

45
00:02:56,250 --> 00:02:58,650
and instead has inconsistent,

46
00:02:58,650 --> 00:03:02,463
intransitive or cyclic preferences.

47
00:03:05,550 --> 00:03:07,860
Arrows, however, are not the only way

48
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:12,860
in which we can represent preferences.

49
00:03:13,350 --> 00:03:18,350
It is typically better to
represent preferences in ranking.

50
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:20,130
For example, if I am the consumer

51
00:03:20,130 --> 00:03:22,950
that prefers banana over
the other two fruits,

52
00:03:22,950 --> 00:03:26,790
I will have banana as my first option.

53
00:03:26,790 --> 00:03:28,620
If there are other
fruits in consideration,

54
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:33,360
I could have considered mango.

55
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:35,130
The next position in this example,

56
00:03:35,130 --> 00:03:36,510
it will be the third position,

57
00:03:36,510 --> 00:03:40,470
assuming that there are other
fruits in the possibility

58
00:03:40,470 --> 00:03:45,470
and apple would be my
fifth favorite fruit.

59
00:03:46,440 --> 00:03:51,440
So ranking order also can
represent preferences,

60
00:03:52,710 --> 00:03:55,410
not only arrows as we were saying before.

61
00:03:55,410 --> 00:03:56,820
But there is a third way,

62
00:03:56,820 --> 00:03:58,980
which is actually the way we will discuss

63
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:02,490
and we will use pretty much
for most of this class,

64
00:04:02,490 --> 00:04:04,260
which is satisfaction levels

65
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:07,020
that we will call later utility level.

66
00:04:07,020 --> 00:04:11,160
So it could be that if
I prefer banana most,

67
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:15,000
then I will give the
highest satisfaction level,

68
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,540
let's say in this case 10
and I will have smaller

69
00:04:18,540 --> 00:04:21,660
or lower satisfaction
levels to the other options.

70
00:04:21,660 --> 00:04:24,240
For example, mango will receive eight

71
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:26,223
and apple will receive six.

72
00:04:29,910 --> 00:04:32,700
Let us now do some notation.

73
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:35,130
So we will use some notation to simplify

74
00:04:35,130 --> 00:04:40,130
some of the math we will
discuss in the examples.

75
00:04:40,230 --> 00:04:44,490
But in general, it's good to
know how the economist language

76
00:04:44,490 --> 00:04:49,490
or logical and mathematical
language operates

77
00:04:50,250 --> 00:04:55,050
to be able to read some of the discussion

78
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:57,480
in the textbook and outside.

79
00:04:57,480 --> 00:05:02,040
So the first important
notation we need to learn today

80
00:05:02,040 --> 00:05:07,040
is about how we write or how
we denote strict preferences.

81
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:11,910
We're gonna denote strict
preferences with a symbol.

82
00:05:11,910 --> 00:05:14,310
For example, if we want to say x

83
00:05:14,310 --> 00:05:18,330
is strictly more preferred than y,

84
00:05:18,330 --> 00:05:22,980
it's gonna be written in this thing

85
00:05:22,980 --> 00:05:27,540
that is right now in a light blue frame.

86
00:05:27,540 --> 00:05:31,440
If we want to denote weak preference,

87
00:05:31,440 --> 00:05:33,630
meaning, saying for example,

88
00:05:33,630 --> 00:05:36,243
x is as least as preferred as y,

89
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:40,410
then we will have this similar symbol,

90
00:05:40,410 --> 00:05:43,710
but with one squiggly thin below

91
00:05:43,710 --> 00:05:48,150
as it is now highlighted on the screen.

92
00:05:48,150 --> 00:05:50,250
If we instead want to denote indifference,

93
00:05:50,250 --> 00:05:53,760
we will use this tilde symbol

94
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:58,620
and this tilde symbol
represents the statement,

95
00:05:58,620 --> 00:06:01,173
x is equally preferred as y.

96
00:06:03,330 --> 00:06:05,700
Now we're going to
discuss some assumptions

97
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:10,350
that we economists impose or have

98
00:06:10,350 --> 00:06:14,520
for the preference relations

99
00:06:14,520 --> 00:06:16,983
and in general, for consumers' choice.

100
00:06:22,252 --> 00:06:26,010
The first assumption,
it's called completeness.

101
00:06:26,010 --> 00:06:30,180
Completeness is, in colloquial terms,

102
00:06:30,180 --> 00:06:34,593
is the idea that the
consumer, our consumer,

103
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:41,600
is always able to answer
a preference question.

104
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:43,860
What is a preference question?

105
00:06:43,860 --> 00:06:46,590
A preference question
is a question such that,

106
00:06:46,590 --> 00:06:51,590
do you prefer iPhone or Samsung Galaxy?

107
00:06:52,140 --> 00:06:55,500
Do you prefer apple or banana?

108
00:06:55,500 --> 00:06:57,270
Those are preference questions

109
00:06:57,270 --> 00:07:01,080
and completeness assumption
says that or states

110
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:06,080
that the consumer is always
able to answer something,

111
00:07:06,330 --> 00:07:10,860
meaning saying, "I prefer
iPhone to Samsung Galaxy,"

112
00:07:10,860 --> 00:07:13,920
or, "I prefer Samsung Galaxy to iPhone,"

113
00:07:13,920 --> 00:07:16,830
or, "I am indifferent between those two."

114
00:07:16,830 --> 00:07:19,530
In simplified terms, this
is equivalent to say,

115
00:07:19,530 --> 00:07:22,440
the consumer should be able to say always,

116
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:25,620
whether x is at least as good

117
00:07:25,620 --> 00:07:30,540
or option x is at least
as good as option Y

118
00:07:30,540 --> 00:07:35,310
or the opposite or the converse statement,

119
00:07:35,310 --> 00:07:40,230
which is that y is at
least as good as option x.

120
00:07:40,230 --> 00:07:43,770
So the ability of the consumer to be able

121
00:07:43,770 --> 00:07:48,770
to answer the question with this or this

122
00:07:48,870 --> 00:07:51,870
is the assumption of completeness,

123
00:07:51,870 --> 00:07:54,153
the consumer is always able to say.

124
00:07:55,020 --> 00:07:57,097
So students sometimes ask me, say,

125
00:07:57,097 --> 00:08:00,093
"Wait, that sounds very broad.

126
00:08:01,440 --> 00:08:02,760
I mean, I don't see a situation

127
00:08:02,760 --> 00:08:06,810
in which you will not
satisfy completeness."

128
00:08:06,810 --> 00:08:08,610
And I'll give you a simple example

129
00:08:08,610 --> 00:08:13,110
of how we would not satisfy completeness.

130
00:08:13,110 --> 00:08:16,710
Suppose I ask you, "Do you prefer

131
00:08:16,710 --> 00:08:21,180
two burritos or two slices of pizza?"

132
00:08:21,180 --> 00:08:25,440
And you tell me like, "I don't now."

133
00:08:25,440 --> 00:08:28,080
So you don't tell me, "I'm indifferent,"

134
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:29,850
you don't tell me, "I
prefer this to that,"

135
00:08:29,850 --> 00:08:32,490
or, "That to this," you
tell me, "I don't know.

136
00:08:32,490 --> 00:08:36,420
I do not know how to answer
that preference question."

137
00:08:36,420 --> 00:08:41,370
If you really do not know
how to answer that question,

138
00:08:41,370 --> 00:08:43,890
then you indeed are violating

139
00:08:43,890 --> 00:08:46,110
the assumption of completeness.

140
00:08:46,110 --> 00:08:49,830
So in our words, in
economics, completeness means

141
00:08:49,830 --> 00:08:53,280
that we always assume that
the consumer always knows

142
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:55,590
something about its preferences

143
00:08:55,590 --> 00:08:59,310
to be able to say something regarding

144
00:08:59,310 --> 00:09:01,770
or along the lines of,
"I prefer this to that

145
00:09:01,770 --> 00:09:04,347
or that to this or those are indifferent."

146
00:09:05,190 --> 00:09:08,163
The next assumption and possibly,

147
00:09:09,480 --> 00:09:14,250
the most important assumption,
it's called transitivity.

148
00:09:14,250 --> 00:09:19,250
Transitivity is a consistency assumption.

149
00:09:19,290 --> 00:09:24,290
It's an assumption that economists have

150
00:09:24,450 --> 00:09:25,907
to provide

151
00:09:30,330 --> 00:09:35,057
our morals with consistent
rational agents.

152
00:09:38,430 --> 00:09:41,130
It is true that not everyone is rational,

153
00:09:41,130 --> 00:09:46,050
but it's also true that most people

154
00:09:46,050 --> 00:09:48,600
or a large portion of the populations

155
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:51,420
are relatively rational.

156
00:09:51,420 --> 00:09:55,830
So transitivity consistency,
which is related to rationality

157
00:09:55,830 --> 00:09:59,160
is a very important
assumption in that regard.

158
00:09:59,160 --> 00:10:01,080
But what exactly is it?

159
00:10:01,080 --> 00:10:04,830
What exactly transitivity assumption is?

160
00:10:04,830 --> 00:10:07,860
So in the transitivity assumption,

161
00:10:07,860 --> 00:10:12,750
we will rule out that consumers
have cyclic preferences.

162
00:10:12,750 --> 00:10:14,610
How do we do that?

163
00:10:14,610 --> 00:10:17,640
We say, suppose there are three goods.

164
00:10:17,640 --> 00:10:20,940
Or sorry, suppose there are three options.

165
00:10:20,940 --> 00:10:23,460
You can take three different options.

166
00:10:23,460 --> 00:10:25,080
They can be three different bundles

167
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:28,290
or they can be three
different fruits or something.

168
00:10:28,290 --> 00:10:31,353
There are three different choice options.

169
00:10:32,280 --> 00:10:36,243
If we denote these three options as x,

170
00:10:38,340 --> 00:10:41,250
whoops, I couldn't highlight, but okay.

171
00:10:41,250 --> 00:10:45,033
If we denote these three
options are x, y and z,

172
00:10:46,050 --> 00:10:50,880
we should have that always
the following statement holds.

173
00:10:50,880 --> 00:10:54,480
If x is at least as good as y

174
00:10:54,480 --> 00:10:57,390
and y is at least as good as z,

175
00:10:57,390 --> 00:11:02,390
then it must be that x is
also at least as good as z.

176
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:11,720
So let's actually do, sorry about that.

177
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:14,227
Let's actually do some example.

178
00:11:19,440 --> 00:11:24,440
So suppose let's say that x is the iPhone

179
00:11:33,240 --> 00:11:37,643
and y is Samsung Galaxy

180
00:11:40,110 --> 00:11:45,110
and z is, let's say, a Nokia 900.

181
00:12:02,010 --> 00:12:07,010
Suppose this consumer
weakly prefers x to y,

182
00:12:08,280 --> 00:12:11,850
meaning iPhone to the Samsung Galaxy,

183
00:12:11,850 --> 00:12:16,850
and then also prefers the
Samsung Galaxy to the Nokia 900,

184
00:12:23,400 --> 00:12:28,400
it must be that the iPhone

185
00:12:34,470 --> 00:12:38,497
is also weakly preferred to the Nokia 900.

186
00:12:40,590 --> 00:12:42,693
So if this is correct,

187
00:12:43,620 --> 00:12:45,870
if this is indeed what happens,

188
00:12:45,870 --> 00:12:49,923
then we say, then transitivity assumption,

189
00:12:51,232 --> 00:12:56,232
transitivity, holds.

190
00:13:00,390 --> 00:13:05,390
So that is a simple
assumption, a simple example.

191
00:13:07,110 --> 00:13:09,903
Now let's move on to the next assumption.

192
00:13:12,240 --> 00:13:17,240
The next assumption is,
we call it, monotonicity

193
00:13:17,250 --> 00:13:21,033
or simply more is better.

194
00:13:22,200 --> 00:13:26,010
This assumption of
monotonicity or more is better

195
00:13:26,010 --> 00:13:31,010
is simply saying that if
everything is the same

196
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:36,990
between two bundles,
except in one of them,

197
00:13:36,990 --> 00:13:40,140
there is more of one of the goods,

198
00:13:40,140 --> 00:13:44,490
then the one bundle that
has more of one of the goods

199
00:13:44,490 --> 00:13:47,610
is strictly preferred to the order bundle.

200
00:13:47,610 --> 00:13:49,290
So here's an example.

201
00:13:49,290 --> 00:13:53,070
Suppose we have the bundle 5, 20,

202
00:13:53,070 --> 00:13:55,560
Five ounces of coffee

203
00:13:55,560 --> 00:13:59,280
and 20 cups of orange juice, let's say.

204
00:13:59,280 --> 00:14:02,673
So five ounces of coffee
and 20 cups of orange juice.

205
00:14:06,090 --> 00:14:09,870
Suppose now we have
slightly different bundle

206
00:14:09,870 --> 00:14:14,520
that has 5.01 ounces of coffee

207
00:14:14,520 --> 00:14:18,993
and still 20 cups of orange juice.

208
00:14:21,480 --> 00:14:26,480
We will assume that
just the fact of having

209
00:14:26,490 --> 00:14:28,680
slightly more of one of the goods,

210
00:14:28,680 --> 00:14:31,230
of at least of one of the goods,

211
00:14:31,230 --> 00:14:34,320
then will give the second bundle,

212
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:36,360
the one that has a bit more coffee,

213
00:14:36,360 --> 00:14:40,020
the property to be strictly prefer

214
00:14:40,020 --> 00:14:44,520
over the original or the initial bundle.

215
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:48,870
If a consumer preference
is word like this,

216
00:14:48,870 --> 00:14:51,630
then we say that this consumer satisfy

217
00:14:51,630 --> 00:14:55,440
the more is better or
monotonicity assumption.

218
00:14:55,440 --> 00:14:58,503
So let me ask you a
couple of questions here.

219
00:14:59,370 --> 00:15:00,513
So first question,

220
00:15:02,880 --> 00:15:06,663
if the assumption of monotonicity holds,

221
00:15:07,590 --> 00:15:11,910
which bundle is preferred, D or C?

222
00:15:11,910 --> 00:15:16,910
D as in duck or C as in cat?

223
00:15:22,020 --> 00:15:24,723
I hope you answered this correctly.

224
00:15:26,211 --> 00:15:28,110
The answer is D, because in D,

225
00:15:28,110 --> 00:15:30,420
we have not only more of one good,

226
00:15:30,420 --> 00:15:32,230
we have more of both goods

227
00:15:33,327 --> 00:15:36,330
with respect to C, so that is better.

228
00:15:36,330 --> 00:15:41,330
Let's move, for example E
here, right to the border

229
00:15:44,250 --> 00:15:49,250
and ask what would be the
comparison between E and C?

230
00:15:50,100 --> 00:15:54,390
And the answer would be E
would be strictly prefer to C,

231
00:15:54,390 --> 00:15:58,410
because although it might have
the same amount of good two,

232
00:15:58,410 --> 00:16:00,963
it has more of good one.

233
00:16:02,310 --> 00:16:07,310
And similar, you could
ask, what is the relation

234
00:16:07,530 --> 00:16:12,480
between C versus B under
the monotonicity assumption?

235
00:16:12,480 --> 00:16:15,060
And the answer would be that,

236
00:16:15,060 --> 00:16:17,343
I'll give you a second to think about.

237
00:16:19,740 --> 00:16:23,190
The answer would be that we don't know.

238
00:16:23,190 --> 00:16:26,610
The monotonicity assumption

239
00:16:26,610 --> 00:16:30,240
does not tell anything
about between C and B.

240
00:16:30,240 --> 00:16:34,953
It does tell things about
D versus C, E versus C.

241
00:16:36,120 --> 00:16:39,360
Monotonicity assumption does not solve

242
00:16:39,360 --> 00:16:42,150
all the ranking of preferences.

243
00:16:42,150 --> 00:16:47,150
Just gives you some of those comparisons

244
00:16:47,610 --> 00:16:51,330
and in this case, it does
answer the comparison

245
00:16:51,330 --> 00:16:54,656
between D and C because
D has more of everything.

246
00:16:54,656 --> 00:16:57,150
It helps you with comparing E and C,

247
00:16:57,150 --> 00:17:02,040
because E has more of one good

248
00:17:02,040 --> 00:17:07,040
and the same of the other
good with respect to bundle C,

249
00:17:07,230 --> 00:17:12,230
but does not rank the
order between A, B and C.

250
00:17:13,980 --> 00:17:17,070
Because between B and C, for example,

251
00:17:17,070 --> 00:17:19,770
indeed B has more than one of the goods,

252
00:17:19,770 --> 00:17:21,300
but has less of the other,

253
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:24,060
so we cannot apply the same logic

254
00:17:24,060 --> 00:17:27,319
as we did between D and C.

255
00:17:27,319 --> 00:17:30,094
(intriguing music)

